## Quickstart

```bash
cp .env.example .env
cp docker-compose.override.yml.example docker-compose.override.yml
{% if use_caddy %}docker network create caddy_net   # once per host
{% endif %}
docker compose up --build
```

{% if use_shinobi %}
The API is live at `http://localhost:8000/api/v1/health`.
{% else %}
The admin site is at `http://localhost:8000/admin/`.
{% endif %}
{% if use_caddy %}
In production, Caddy fronts `{{ domain_name }}` and handles HTTPS automatically
(Let's Encrypt) — see `Caddyfile`. It runs standalone, independent of
`docker compose up` above:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.caddy.yml up -d
```

See `docs/architecture.md` if you're running more than one project from this
template on the same host — only one of them should start
`docker-compose.caddy.yml`. Locally, `docker-compose.override.yml` publishes
the app's port directly instead, since Caddy's automatic HTTPS needs a real
public domain and inbound 80/443.
{% endif %}

## Local (non-Docker) dev

```bash
uv sync --all-extras --dev
uv run pre-commit install
uv run python manage.py migrate
uv run python manage.py runserver
```

If you want local email capture on that path, run `docker compose up mailpit` in a
second terminal. The generated settings default SMTP to `localhost:1025`, which the
compose `mailpit` service publishes on the host, and its web UI is at
`http://localhost:8025`.

## Tests

